    "q-162776": {
      "dom_id": "step-q162776",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)=10x-2\\sqrt{15}y\\,$ on the ellipse $5{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=5.$",
      "worked_solution": "We must find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=5{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=5.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=5\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨10,-2\\sqrt{15}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨10x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}10=10\\lambda x \\\\ -2\\sqrt{15}=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{1}{\\lambda } \\\\ y=-\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ and $y$ into the condition $g(x,y)=5$ and solve for $\\lambda ,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\cdot \\frac{1}{{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{15}{{\\lambda }^{2}} & =5 \\\\ \\frac{5+15}{{\\lambda }^{2}} & =5 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =4 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $x$ and $y.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx=\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,y=∓\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2}),\\,(\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2})\\,$ | $\\,(\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2})\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,-20\\,$ | $\\,20\\,$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{2}),$ and its minimum value is $-20.$",
