    "q-99030": {
      "dom_id": "step-q99030",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)=3x-y+6\\,$ on the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=10.$",
      "worked_solution": "We must find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=10.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=10\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨3,-1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}3=2\\lambda x \\\\ -1=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{3}{2\\lambda } \\\\ y=-\\frac{1}{2\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ and $y$ into the condition $g(x,y)=10$ and solve for $\\lambda ,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}} & =10 \\\\ \\frac{10}{4{\\lambda }^{2}} & =10 \\\\ 4{\\lambda }^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $x$ and $y.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx=\\pm 3,\\,y=∓1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-3,1),\\,(3,-1).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,(-3,1)\\,$ | $\\,(3,-1)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,-4\\,$ | $\\,16\\,$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(3,-1),$ and its maximum value is $16.$",
