    "q-162791": {
      "dom_id": "step-q162791",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)=-4x+y+4\\,$ on the parabola $8x-{y}^{2}=1.$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that the global maximum of $f(x,y)$ exists on the parabola.*",
      "worked_solution": "We must find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=8x-{y}^{2}=1.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=1\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨-4,1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨8,-2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}-4=8\\lambda  \\\\ 1=-2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ y=-\\frac{1}{2\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now use $\\lambda $ to find $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{2(-\\frac{1}{2})}=1.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expression for $y$ into the condition $g(x,y)=1$ and solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8x-{y}^{2}=1 \\\\ 8x-1 & =1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has a possible extreme value at the point\n\n$$\n(\\frac{1}{4},1).\n$$\n\nSince the given parabola opens to the right, there isn't a minimum value of $f$ on the parabola. Thus, the extreme value is the maximum of $f$ on the parabola. We evaluate $f$ at this point:\n\n| $\\,(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,(\\frac{1}{4},1)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,4\\,$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(\\frac{1}{4},1),$ and its maximum value is $4.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
