    "q-162769": {
      "dom_id": "step-q162769",
      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)=2x-{y}^{2}\\,$ on the hyperbola ${y}^{2}-{x}^{2}=3.$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the global maximum of $f(x,y)$ exists on the hyperbola.*",
      "worked_solution": "We must find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}-3=0.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=0\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2,-2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨-2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}2=-2\\lambda x \\\\ -2y=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}1-x\\lambda =0 \\\\ y(\\lambda +1)=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $y=0$ in the second equation of the system, the constraint condition ${y}^{2}-{x}^{2}-3=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}-3 & =0\\,⇒\\,{x}^{2}=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives a contradiction.\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =-1$ in the second equation of the system, the first equation of the system implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+x(-1)=0\\,⇒\\,x=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the constraint condition ${y}^{2}-{x}^{2}-3=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-4 & =0\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(-1,-2),(1,2).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(1,-2),\\,(1,2).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(1,-2)$ | $(1,2)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-2$ | $-2$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at the points $(1,\\pm 2)$ and its maximum value is $-2.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
