    "q-162784": {
      "dom_id": "step-q162784",
      "name": "Question 11",
      "problem": "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}+3$ on the surface ${z}^{2}-xy-4=0.$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the global minimum of $f(x,y,z)$ exists on the surface.*",
      "worked_solution": "We need to find the minimum value of $f$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y,z)={z}^{2}-xy-4=0.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=0\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,2y,2z⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨-y,-x,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x=-\\lambda y \\\\ 2y=-\\lambda x \\\\ 2z=2\\lambda z\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}(4-{\\lambda }^{2})x=0 \\\\ (4-{\\lambda }^{2})y=0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )z=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- First, assume that $\\lambda \\ne 1$ and $\\lambda \\ne \\pm 2.$ Then, the solution of the above system is $x=y=z=0.$ But this contradicts the constraint $g(x,y,z)=0.$\n\n- Next, assume that $\\lambda =-2.$ Then we must have $x=y$ and $z=0.$ But this also contradicts the constraint $g(x,y,z)=0.$\n\n- Now, assume that $\\lambda =1.$ Then, by the first and second equations, we must have $x=y=0.$ Also, by the constraint $g(x,y,z)=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}-4=0\\,⇒\\,z=\\pm 2.\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,0,\\pm 2).$\n\n- Finally, assume that $\\lambda =2.$ Then we must have $x=-y$ and $z=0.$ Also, by the constraint $g(x,y,z)=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n-(-y)y-4=0\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 2.\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(∓2,\\pm 2,0).$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,(0,0,\\pm 2)\\,$ | $\\,(∓2,\\pm 2,0)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,7\\,$ | $\\,11\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at the points $(0,0,\\pm 2),$ and its minimum value is $7.$",
