    "q-162773": {
      "dom_id": "step-q162773",
      "name": "Question 12",
      "problem": "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ on the ellipsoid $9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=36.$",
      "worked_solution": "We need to find the minimum value of $f$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y,z)=9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=36.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=36\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,2y,2z⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨18x,8y,2z⟩ \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨9x,4y,z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=9\\lambda x \\\\ y=4\\lambda y \\\\ z=\\lambda z\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}(1-9\\lambda )x=0 \\\\ (1-4\\lambda )y=0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )z=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- First, assume that $\\lambda \\ne \\frac{1}{9},$ $\\lambda \\ne \\frac{1}{4}$ and $\\lambda \\ne 1.$ Then, the solution of the above system is $x=y=z=0.$ But this contradicts the constraint $g(x,y,z)=36.$\n\n- Next, assume that $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{9}.$ Then, by the second and third equations, we must have $y=z=0.$ Also, by the constraint $g(x,y,z)=36,$ we have\n\n$$\n9{x}^{2}=36\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm 2.\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(\\pm 2,0,0).$\n\n- Now, assume that $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{4}.$ Then, by the first and third equations, we must have $x=z=0.$ Also, by the constraint $g(x,y,z)=36,$ we have\n\n$$\n4{y}^{2}=36\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 3.\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,\\pm 3,0).$\n\n- Finally, assume that $\\lambda =1.$ Then, by the first and second equations, we must have $x=y=0.$ Also, by the constraint $g(x,y,z)=36,$ we have\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}=36\\,⇒\\,z=\\pm 6.\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,0,\\pm 6).$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,(\\pm 2,0,0)\\,$ | $\\,(0,\\pm 3,0)\\,$ | $\\,(0,0,\\pm 6)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,4\\,$ | $\\,9\\,$ | $\\,36\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at the points $(\\pm 2,0,0),$ and its minimum value is $4.$",
