    "q-99168": {
      "dom_id": "step-q99168",
      "name": "Question 13",
      "problem": "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}-yz$ on the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1.$",
      "worked_solution": "We need to find the maximum value of $f$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=1\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,-z,-y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x=2\\lambda x \\\\ -z=2\\lambda y \\\\ -y=2\\lambda z\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x(\\lambda -1)=0 \\\\ y(4{\\lambda }^{2}-1)=0 \\\\ z=-2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- First, assume that $\\lambda \\ne 1$ and $\\lambda \\ne \\pm \\frac{1}{2}.$ Then, the solution of the above system is $x=y=z=0.$ But this contradicts the constraint $g(x,y,z)=1.$\n\n- Next, assume that $\\lambda =1.$ Then, by the second and third equations, we must have $y=z=0.$ Also, by the constraint $g(x,y,z)=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=1\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm 1.\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical point $(\\pm 1,0,0).$\n\n- Finally, assume that $\\lambda =\\pm \\frac{1}{2}.$ Then, by the first and third equations, we must have $x=0$ and $z=∓y.$ Also, by the constraint $g(x,y,z)=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+(-y)^{2}=1\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding values of $z$ are\n\n$$\nz=\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}),(0,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}).$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,(0,-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})\\,$ | $\\,(0,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})\\,$ | $\\,(\\pm 1,0,0)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{2}\\,$ | $\\,∓\\frac{1}{2}\\,$ | $\\,1\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at the points $(\\pm 1,0,0),$  and its maximum value is $1.$",
