    "q-99171": {
      "dom_id": "step-q99171",
      "name": "Question 15",
      "problem": "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y,z)=2x+6y+10z$ on the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=35.$",
      "worked_solution": "We need to find the maximum value of $f$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=35.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=35\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2,6,10⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2=2\\lambda x \\\\ 6=2\\lambda y \\\\ 10=2\\lambda z\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{1}{\\lambda } \\\\ y=\\frac{3}{\\lambda } \\\\ z=\\frac{5}{\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x,\\,y$ and $z$ into the condition $g(x,y,z)=35$ and solve for $\\lambda ,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =35 \\\\ \\frac{1}{{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{9}{{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{25}{{\\lambda }^{2}} & =35 \\\\ \\frac{35}{{\\lambda }^{2}} & =35 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $x,\\,y$ and $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx=\\pm 1\\,y=\\pm 3\\,z=\\pm 5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-1,-3,-5),\\,(1,3,5).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,(-1,-3,-5)\\,$ | $\\,(1,3,5)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,-70\\,$ | $\\,70\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(1,3,5),$ and its maximum value is $70.$",
